WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.968 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.968 --> 00:00:04.428 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:04.428 --> 00:00:05.904 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:05.904 --> 00:00:12.573 align:middle line:90%


00:00:12.573 --> 00:00:13.740 align:middle line:90%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: All right.

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:16.030 align:middle line:90%
I guess we'll get started.

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:16.530 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:20.510 align:middle line:84%
I wanted to just finish up
some discussion of topics

00:00:20.510 --> 00:00:24.080 align:middle line:84%
from linear algebra
that we didn't

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:27.120 align:middle line:90%
get to the last math lecture.

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:30.950 align:middle line:84%
And to begin with,
I think we did

00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:35.750 align:middle line:84%
cover this topic of
eigenvalues and eigenvectors.

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:39.740 align:middle line:84%
These linear algebra
concepts turn out

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:42.140 align:middle line:84%
to be very, very
useful in a number

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:45.360 align:middle line:90%
of different applications.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:53.660 align:middle line:84%
But when we have an eigenvalue
and eigenvector pair, then,

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:58.960 align:middle line:84%
for a matrix A that
has an eigenvector V,

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:05.770 align:middle line:84%
then the matrix-vector
product is simply a rescaling

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:07.100 align:middle line:90%
of that same vector.

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:12.040 align:middle line:84%
So matrix multiplication,
when we use eigen--

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:14.980 align:middle line:84%
or matrix-vector multiplication
when we use eigenvectors

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:16.190 align:middle line:90%
is very simple.

00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:18.910 align:middle line:90%
We're just rescaling the input.

00:01:18.910 --> 00:01:25.360 align:middle line:84%
And in order to solve for
eigenvalues and eigenvectors,

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:31.750 align:middle line:84%
one can solve this equation
where the two are equal.

00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:39.040 align:middle line:84%
And when we solve
this equation, we

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:46.780 align:middle line:84%
want the roots of the
determinant equation to equal 0.

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:52.030 align:middle line:84%
This means that these
are the eigenvalues which

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:59.190 align:middle line:84%
make this matrix factor, A
minus I lambda, non-invertible.

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:06.120 align:middle line:84%
So with a matrix, if that matrix
has a determinant that is--

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:11.520 align:middle line:84%
or if that matrix is
invertible, then the determinant

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:14.760 align:middle line:84%
of the matrix is
nonzero, and the inverse

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:19.200 align:middle line:84%
is actually functions of
different determinant cofactors.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:23.850 align:middle line:84%
So in order for v
to not be 0, which

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:27.600 align:middle line:84%
would be the case if this
matrix were invertible,

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:31.090 align:middle line:84%
we must have the lambda
satisfying this equation.

00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:38.740 align:middle line:84%
And this system of equations
is a polynomial in lambda,

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:42.600 align:middle line:84%
or at least, the determinant
equaling 0 corresponds

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:46.060 align:middle line:84%
to a polynomial in
lambda equaling 0.

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:51.300 align:middle line:84%
So these things can be
solved very directly.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:54.540 align:middle line:84%
Actually, in R, you can
actually compute the roots

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:56.640 align:middle line:90%
of any polynomial very easily.

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:01.170 align:middle line:84%
And then, once we've
solved for our root lambda,

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:03.920 align:middle line:84%
then we can solve the
equations for what the v

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:06.240 align:middle line:90%
vector is that satisfies that.

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:10.285 align:middle line:90%
Now, let me put this in here.

00:03:10.285 --> 00:03:15.335 align:middle line:90%


00:03:15.335 --> 00:03:24.410 align:middle line:84%
It turns out that if we have
independent eigenvectors, then

00:03:24.410 --> 00:03:25.940 align:middle line:90%
we can--

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:29.210 align:middle line:84%
linearly independent
eigenvectors--

00:03:29.210 --> 00:03:36.200 align:middle line:84%
then we can comprise those
as columns of a matrix S.

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:41.810 align:middle line:84%
And the matrix A
multiplied by this S matrix

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:47.010 align:middle line:84%
will simply be the matrix of
multiples of the eigenvectors,

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:48.240 align:middle line:90%
so S lambda.

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:50.450 align:middle line:90%
Lambda is a diagonal matrix.

00:03:50.450 --> 00:03:57.050 align:middle line:84%
And the inverse
matrix of S will exist

00:03:57.050 --> 00:04:01.060 align:middle line:84%
so long as we have
independent eigenvectors.

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:06.400 align:middle line:84%
And so this equation
here can be used

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:10.480 align:middle line:84%
to obtain A is S
lambda S inverse.

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:14.840 align:middle line:84%
Alternatively, S inverse
A S is equal to lambda.

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:21.430 align:middle line:84%
So these eigenvectors,
when comprising a matrix,

00:04:21.430 --> 00:04:28.960 align:middle line:84%
allow us to
diagonalize a matrix A.

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:33.340 align:middle line:90%
Now, let's see.

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:36.610 align:middle line:84%
All right, so
eigenvalues, eigenvectors,

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:41.980 align:middle line:84%
can come into play when we have
equations for Kalman filters.

00:04:41.980 --> 00:04:48.700 align:middle line:84%
If our matrix A is transforming
states at time t minus 1

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:53.740 align:middle line:84%
into states at time t, and
the dynamics of this process

00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:58.370 align:middle line:84%
proceed regularly over
time increments given by t,

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:03.820 align:middle line:84%
then we simply have, the
t-th transition from state 0

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:08.260 align:middle line:90%
is given by the t-th power of A.

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:12.730 align:middle line:84%
And we can actually get
explicit solutions, then,

00:05:12.730 --> 00:05:17.860 align:middle line:90%
which end up giving us--

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:18.970 align:middle line:90%
let's see.

00:05:18.970 --> 00:05:23.200 align:middle line:84%
At least, if our
eigenvectors, v1 through vn,

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:30.370 align:middle line:84%
are linearly independent, then,
if we have an initial condition,

00:05:30.370 --> 00:05:34.000 align:middle line:84%
u0, we can express
that initial condition

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:37.960 align:middle line:84%
as a linear combination
of the eigenvectors.

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:43.750 align:middle line:84%
And then we can represent
the transition over t periods

00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:45.890 align:middle line:90%
to have this form.

00:05:45.890 --> 00:05:49.240 align:middle line:84%
And it ends up just
being a sum of multiples

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:54.420 align:middle line:84%
of the eigenvectors, with
the coefficients being

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:57.160 align:middle line:90%
powers of the eigenvalues.

00:05:57.160 --> 00:06:00.700 align:middle line:84%
So in looking at
this mathematically,

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:07.230 align:middle line:84%
one can see that if the
eigenvalues, lambda 1 to lambda

00:06:07.230 --> 00:06:13.860 align:middle line:84%
n, are smaller than
1 in magnitude, then

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:21.720 align:middle line:84%
this limiting state transition
goes to a zero vector.

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:30.180 align:middle line:84%
If one of the eigenvalues
is equal to 1,

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:35.200 align:middle line:84%
then this term here won't
change with each transition,

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:37.530 align:middle line:84%
and so we'll have
a limiting value

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:39.990 align:middle line:84%
of the state that
corresponds to a multiple

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:41.740 align:middle line:90%
of the first eigenvector.

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:45.780 align:middle line:84%
And so what's
interesting to anticipate

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:48.540 align:middle line:84%
is that there will be
different kinds of problems

00:06:48.540 --> 00:06:56.040 align:middle line:84%
we work with where the
eigenvalues may actually be such

00:06:56.040 --> 00:06:59.370 align:middle line:84%
that the largest
eigenvalue is equal to 1,

00:06:59.370 --> 00:07:04.090 align:middle line:84%
and the other eigenvalues are
smaller than 1 in magnitude.

00:07:04.090 --> 00:07:08.670 align:middle line:84%
And so we can get nice
analytic representations

00:07:08.670 --> 00:07:11.070 align:middle line:84%
for the transition
of the states.

00:07:11.070 --> 00:07:11.800 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:16.320 align:middle line:84%
Well, from linear
algebra courses,

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:22.870 align:middle line:84%
you probably remember that if we
have a symmetric real matrix--

00:07:22.870 --> 00:07:30.600 align:middle line:84%
so if this matrix here is
n by n, and it's symmetric,

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:37.440 align:middle line:84%
and it's real, then all of
the eigenvalues of this matrix

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:40.320 align:middle line:90%
are real as well.

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:47.200 align:middle line:84%
And if we have two
distinct eigenvalues,

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:51.070 align:middle line:84%
then the eigenvectors
corresponding

00:07:51.070 --> 00:07:57.290 align:middle line:84%
to those two eigenvalues are
orthogonal to each other.

00:07:57.290 --> 00:08:04.180 align:middle line:84%
So if we have A v1
equals lambda 1 v1,

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:10.510 align:middle line:84%
and A v2 is equal
to lambda 2 v2,

00:08:10.510 --> 00:08:19.210 align:middle line:84%
then it turns out that
v1 dot v2 is equal to 0.

00:08:19.210 --> 00:08:26.440 align:middle line:90%
And this is useful.

00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:27.700 align:middle line:90%
At least, if we're--

00:08:27.700 --> 00:08:30.640 align:middle line:84%
with linear algebra,
we often want

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:39.220 align:middle line:84%
to represent multidimensional
vectors in terms of some basis.

00:08:39.220 --> 00:08:48.960 align:middle line:84%
And so, if we think of our
matrix A equaling a vector a1,

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:59.940 align:middle line:84%
a2, up to a n, if the
matrix A has full rank,

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:06.750 align:middle line:84%
then these columns of A
are linearly independent.

00:09:06.750 --> 00:09:11.850 align:middle line:84%
And we could use
these column vectors

00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:15.640 align:middle line:84%
as a basis for the
n-dimensional space.

00:09:15.640 --> 00:09:23.140 align:middle line:84%
So if the rank of
A is equal to n,

00:09:23.140 --> 00:09:30.483 align:middle line:84%
then the collection of aj's, j
equaling 1 to n, forms a basis.

00:09:30.483 --> 00:09:33.360 align:middle line:90%


00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:42.770 align:middle line:84%
And if we have a matrix A
that maybe is symmetric,

00:09:42.770 --> 00:09:47.070 align:middle line:84%
but has eigenvalues
that are all distinct,

00:09:47.070 --> 00:09:50.730 align:middle line:90%
then we actually could have--

00:09:50.730 --> 00:10:02.960 align:middle line:84%
so if A is symmetric, and all
the eigenvalues are distinct,

00:10:02.960 --> 00:10:09.575 align:middle line:84%
then the collection
of eigenvectors, vj,

00:10:09.575 --> 00:10:14.210 align:middle line:84%
j equaling 1 to n,
this would correspond

00:10:14.210 --> 00:10:18.350 align:middle line:90%
to an orthogonal basis.

00:10:18.350 --> 00:10:23.060 align:middle line:84%
And so, when
representing n vectors,

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:29.210 align:middle line:84%
it's convenient to represent
them in a coordinate system that

00:10:29.210 --> 00:10:33.500 align:middle line:90%
has orthogonal axes in it.

00:10:33.500 --> 00:10:43.330 align:middle line:84%
Now, what's interesting is that
when A is symmetric and real,

00:10:43.330 --> 00:10:45.560 align:middle line:90%
all the eigenvalues are real.

00:10:45.560 --> 00:10:52.270 align:middle line:84%
It turns out that there
exists an orthogonal set

00:10:52.270 --> 00:10:57.910 align:middle line:84%
of eigenvectors in
that general case.

00:10:57.910 --> 00:11:01.690 align:middle line:90%
So that can be very useful.

00:11:01.690 --> 00:11:03.130 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:11:03.130 --> 00:11:09.760 align:middle line:84%
Well, a very important
linear algebra result

00:11:09.760 --> 00:11:13.160 align:middle line:84%
is the singular
value decomposition.

00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:17.020 align:middle line:84%
And let me just ask, how
many people here have covered

00:11:17.020 --> 00:11:19.160 align:middle line:90%
singular value decompositions?

00:11:19.160 --> 00:11:20.985 align:middle line:90%
Just about everybody, OK.

00:11:20.985 --> 00:11:24.790 align:middle line:84%
So who would like to
explain to me what--

00:11:24.790 --> 00:11:28.560 align:middle line:84%
or explain to the class what a
singular value decomposition is?

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:34.690 align:middle line:90%


00:11:34.690 --> 00:11:40.560 align:middle line:84%
So let me write this,
maybe, as the singular value

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:43.560 align:middle line:90%
decomposition in my notation.

00:11:43.560 --> 00:11:48.810 align:middle line:84%
So who would like to
explain what this is?

00:11:48.810 --> 00:11:49.570 align:middle line:90%
Anybody?

00:11:49.570 --> 00:11:52.320 align:middle line:90%


00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:53.350 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:11:53.350 --> 00:11:55.140 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Expresses
the matrix A as a sum

00:11:55.140 --> 00:11:58.680 align:middle line:84%
of orthogonal
[INAUDIBLE] matrices.

00:11:58.680 --> 00:12:04.590 align:middle line:84%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: So u
is such that u transpose

00:12:04.590 --> 00:12:11.820 align:middle line:84%
u is equal to, say, the identity
that has orthogonal columns.

00:12:11.820 --> 00:12:15.780 align:middle line:90%
V is the same thing.

00:12:15.780 --> 00:12:17.253 align:middle line:90%
And D is a diagonal.

00:12:17.253 --> 00:12:21.150 align:middle line:90%


00:12:21.150 --> 00:12:28.920 align:middle line:84%
And so what's important
is that any matrix

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:37.110 align:middle line:84%
can be expressed as a
factor of a V transpose,

00:12:37.110 --> 00:12:42.170 align:middle line:84%
which is an orthogonal matrix,
that will transform coordinate

00:12:42.170 --> 00:12:46.860 align:middle line:84%
axes to new axes that are
orthogonal in the same space.

00:12:46.860 --> 00:12:53.540 align:middle line:84%
And then D is a diagonal
matrix down to d--

00:12:53.540 --> 00:12:55.910 align:middle line:90%
well, I'll write dr--

00:12:55.910 --> 00:13:02.160 align:middle line:84%
where we basically are
stretching and squeezing axes.

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:06.350 align:middle line:84%
And then u simply
has columns that

00:13:06.350 --> 00:13:10.500 align:middle line:84%
define a new basis or
a new vector space.

00:13:10.500 --> 00:13:12.330 align:middle line:90%
And we're taking sums of those.

00:13:12.330 --> 00:13:18.230 align:middle line:84%
So it's interesting to
represent any matrix

00:13:18.230 --> 00:13:26.720 align:middle line:84%
as this operator, which will
do an orthogonal transformation

00:13:26.720 --> 00:13:30.290 align:middle line:84%
of axes, stretch and squeeze
in that transformed space,

00:13:30.290 --> 00:13:37.280 align:middle line:84%
and then use those to multiply
new coordinate vectors, u.

00:13:37.280 --> 00:13:44.210 align:middle line:90%
Now, if we take any--

00:13:44.210 --> 00:13:48.023 align:middle line:84%
let's say, here, an
m by n matrix, A--

00:13:48.023 --> 00:13:53.570 align:middle line:90%


00:13:53.570 --> 00:13:58.610 align:middle line:84%
and it's often the case
that we have maybe m cases

00:13:58.610 --> 00:14:02.660 align:middle line:90%
in a data set and n variables.

00:14:02.660 --> 00:14:06.110 align:middle line:84%
And so this corresponds
to a data matrix

00:14:06.110 --> 00:14:11.450 align:middle line:90%
for an analysis of some data.

00:14:11.450 --> 00:14:17.293 align:middle line:84%
If we take A transpose A, then
this is a symmetric real matrix.

00:14:17.293 --> 00:14:21.830 align:middle line:90%


00:14:21.830 --> 00:14:31.990 align:middle line:84%
And so this actually can be
diagonalized to V D squared V

00:14:31.990 --> 00:14:37.120 align:middle line:84%
transpose, where
I'm representing

00:14:37.120 --> 00:14:43.870 align:middle line:84%
the eigenvalues as
the squared D values

00:14:43.870 --> 00:14:49.090 align:middle line:84%
and the eigenvectors
as columns of V.

00:14:49.090 --> 00:14:54.100 align:middle line:84%
And so one can
construct this, do

00:14:54.100 --> 00:14:57.680 align:middle line:84%
the eigenvalue-eigenvector
decomposition here.

00:14:57.680 --> 00:15:04.189 align:middle line:84%
And then we can take the matrix
A, premultiplied by V, and--

00:15:04.189 --> 00:15:08.101 align:middle line:90%


00:15:08.101 --> 00:15:10.840 align:middle line:84%
or not premultiply--
postmultiply it by V,

00:15:10.840 --> 00:15:13.810 align:middle line:84%
and postmultiply
it by D inverse.

00:15:13.810 --> 00:15:18.290 align:middle line:84%
And this will basically
be our U matrix.

00:15:18.290 --> 00:15:22.563 align:middle line:84%
Now, all of this is a bit
casual with my notation.

00:15:22.563 --> 00:15:25.090 align:middle line:90%


00:15:25.090 --> 00:15:30.670 align:middle line:84%
The number of nonzero
diagonal entries

00:15:30.670 --> 00:15:39.130 align:middle line:84%
here can be limited to the
rank count of the matrix A.

00:15:39.130 --> 00:15:45.310 align:middle line:84%
And so that's the
way it can play out.

00:15:45.310 --> 00:15:50.190 align:middle line:84%
So we basically have
nonzero eigenvalues

00:15:50.190 --> 00:15:53.620 align:middle line:84%
of A transpose A.
There will be r,

00:15:53.620 --> 00:15:57.570 align:middle line:90%
the rank, nonzero eigenvalues.

00:15:57.570 --> 00:16:02.640 align:middle line:84%
We can define the orthogonal,
orthonormal eigenvectors

00:16:02.640 --> 00:16:04.120 align:middle line:90%
corresponding to those.

00:16:04.120 --> 00:16:11.370 align:middle line:84%
And then, if we want to
expand the orthonormal basis

00:16:11.370 --> 00:16:18.600 align:middle line:84%
of the first r eigenvectors
to be an n-dimensional basis,

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:22.140 align:middle line:84%
although they
essentially aren't used,

00:16:22.140 --> 00:16:29.120 align:middle line:84%
and so that gives us our
singular value decomposition.

00:16:29.120 --> 00:16:39.410 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out that in
dealing with data in a matrix,

00:16:39.410 --> 00:16:42.080 align:middle line:84%
the singular value
decomposition allows

00:16:42.080 --> 00:16:45.360 align:middle line:90%
us to reduce dimensionality.

00:16:45.360 --> 00:16:49.670 align:middle line:84%
Perhaps there's variation
in the data values

00:16:49.670 --> 00:16:56.720 align:middle line:84%
that is limited to a
subspace of rank 2 or 3.

00:16:56.720 --> 00:17:02.330 align:middle line:84%
And so we can identify
and specify that subspace

00:17:02.330 --> 00:17:06.140 align:middle line:84%
easily with the singular
value decompositions.

00:17:06.140 --> 00:17:08.720 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:17:08.720 --> 00:17:11.929 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:17:11.929 --> 00:17:16.760 align:middle line:84%
So in these notes, I
refer to reduced SVD.

00:17:16.760 --> 00:17:22.069 align:middle line:84%
What's rather interesting as a
decomposition of the matrix A

00:17:22.069 --> 00:17:26.480 align:middle line:84%
is that it's a sum
of r terms, where

00:17:26.480 --> 00:17:29.090 align:middle line:90%
r is the rank of the matrix.

00:17:29.090 --> 00:17:33.070 align:middle line:84%
And those r terms
are the products

00:17:33.070 --> 00:17:40.870 align:middle line:84%
of the column vectors of u times
the column vector transpose

00:17:40.870 --> 00:17:46.120 align:middle line:84%
of v, respectively,
with the singular value,

00:17:46.120 --> 00:17:48.110 align:middle line:90%
sigma 1 through sigma r.

00:17:48.110 --> 00:17:55.940 align:middle line:84%
So it provides a nice way
of thinking of a matrix.

00:17:55.940 --> 00:18:02.560 align:middle line:84%
It's simply a sum
of rank 1 terms.

00:18:02.560 --> 00:18:04.510 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:18:04.510 --> 00:18:05.140 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:18:05.140 --> 00:18:07.660 align:middle line:84%
These lecture notes on
linear algebra finishes

00:18:07.660 --> 00:18:11.620 align:middle line:84%
with the
Perron-Frobenius theorem.

00:18:11.620 --> 00:18:16.540 align:middle line:84%
And this theorem
relates to matrices

00:18:16.540 --> 00:18:22.870 align:middle line:84%
that are square with
strictly positive elements.

00:18:22.870 --> 00:18:28.470 align:middle line:84%
And so, with such
matrices, the theorem

00:18:28.470 --> 00:18:33.960 align:middle line:84%
states that there is an
eigenvalue, a real eigenvalue,

00:18:33.960 --> 00:18:37.890 align:middle line:84%
lambda 0, that is
bigger in magnitude

00:18:37.890 --> 00:18:41.700 align:middle line:90%
than any other eigenvalues.

00:18:41.700 --> 00:18:47.670 align:middle line:84%
And corresponding to this
eigenvalue, lambda 0,

00:18:47.670 --> 00:18:54.990 align:middle line:84%
we can define an eigenvector
v, which has elements

00:18:54.990 --> 00:18:57.780 align:middle line:90%
that are all positive as well.

00:18:57.780 --> 00:19:04.500 align:middle line:84%
And this lambda 0
eigenvalue, the largest one,

00:19:04.500 --> 00:19:10.260 align:middle line:84%
is alone in terms of,
all other eigenvalues

00:19:10.260 --> 00:19:13.180 align:middle line:90%
are smaller in magnitude.

00:19:13.180 --> 00:19:19.140 align:middle line:84%
And this result turns out
to be very useful in working

00:19:19.140 --> 00:19:21.480 align:middle line:90%
with positive matrices.

00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:24.920 align:middle line:84%
The notes here go through
the proof of that.

00:19:24.920 --> 00:19:27.320 align:middle line:84%
I guess they're
included here, if we

00:19:27.320 --> 00:19:33.140 align:middle line:84%
wanted to get into technical
proofs of linear algebra.

00:19:33.140 --> 00:19:35.300 align:middle line:90%
And I don't want to do that.

00:19:35.300 --> 00:19:36.060 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:19:36.060 --> 00:19:40.850 align:middle line:84%
I finished these notes with just
some mention of different news

00:19:40.850 --> 00:19:48.455 align:middle line:84%
sources that you should
consider exploring in finance.

00:19:48.455 --> 00:19:51.210 align:middle line:90%


00:19:51.210 --> 00:19:52.880 align:middle line:84%
Well, there's Yahoo
Finance, which

00:19:52.880 --> 00:19:57.350 align:middle line:84%
is where we can easily
collect price data.

00:19:57.350 --> 00:20:03.470 align:middle line:84%
But you should know that
the Wall Street Journal is--

00:20:03.470 --> 00:20:05.780 align:middle line:84%
you can get a free
subscription with that

00:20:05.780 --> 00:20:11.010 align:middle line:84%
through your Kerberos
password-- or ID, rather.

00:20:11.010 --> 00:20:12.930 align:middle line:90%
So that's very useful.

00:20:12.930 --> 00:20:14.810 align:middle line:84%
And then there are a
number of other sites

00:20:14.810 --> 00:20:17.700 align:middle line:90%
that you may find useful.

00:20:17.700 --> 00:20:19.580 align:middle line:90%
Now, let's see.

00:20:19.580 --> 00:20:23.020 align:middle line:90%


00:20:23.020 --> 00:20:30.100 align:middle line:84%
In our very first
lecture, I indicated

00:20:30.100 --> 00:20:34.030 align:middle line:84%
how we could use
RStudio to collect

00:20:34.030 --> 00:20:37.180 align:middle line:84%
data, plot time series
of different stocks

00:20:37.180 --> 00:20:38.540 align:middle line:90%
or other assets.

00:20:38.540 --> 00:20:44.890 align:middle line:84%
And in this lecture 2, I
distributed this workspace,

00:20:44.890 --> 00:20:55.660 align:middle line:84%
which has R scripts and output
for looking at portfolios

00:20:55.660 --> 00:20:59.950 align:middle line:84%
of stocks and considering
equal-weighted portfolios

00:20:59.950 --> 00:21:03.680 align:middle line:84%
with different components
in the equal weights.

00:21:03.680 --> 00:21:21.480 align:middle line:84%
And so if you go to the
Rstudio Cloud project--

00:21:21.480 --> 00:21:34.260 align:middle line:84%
so with RStudio Cloud, or
maybe it's Cloud Rstudio,

00:21:34.260 --> 00:21:44.200 align:middle line:84%
one can upload different R
programs in this project.

00:21:44.200 --> 00:21:47.730 align:middle line:84%
And so has anyone
in the class yet

00:21:47.730 --> 00:21:54.490 align:middle line:84%
used RStudio Cloud for
any of the examples?

00:21:54.490 --> 00:21:57.880 align:middle line:84%
I'd like everyone to at least
be capable of doing that.

00:21:57.880 --> 00:22:01.170 align:middle line:84%
And I'd like proof
that it actually

00:22:01.170 --> 00:22:03.100 align:middle line:90%
works with students doing it.

00:22:03.100 --> 00:22:07.590 align:middle line:84%
So it would be great for
some of you to consider that.

00:22:07.590 --> 00:22:12.330 align:middle line:84%
What's nice about
this environment

00:22:12.330 --> 00:22:15.520 align:middle line:84%
is that I can give
you an R program.

00:22:15.520 --> 00:22:19.110 align:middle line:84%
And it should run
just by saying Run.

00:22:19.110 --> 00:22:21.150 align:middle line:84%
You don't have to
install anything.

00:22:21.150 --> 00:22:32.780 align:middle line:84%
And with this output, there was
one program called Script_SP500.

00:22:32.780 --> 00:22:38.090 align:middle line:84%
And there's a table of contents
for this R script, which

00:22:38.090 --> 00:22:41.420 align:middle line:84%
says it loads libraries,
then collects stock prices,

00:22:41.420 --> 00:22:43.820 align:middle line:90%
and then saves the workspace.

00:22:43.820 --> 00:22:47.710 align:middle line:84%
So the commands are all
very simple in here.

00:22:47.710 --> 00:22:50.720 align:middle line:90%


00:22:50.720 --> 00:22:57.510 align:middle line:84%
And then, with the Equal
Weighted Portfolios script,

00:22:57.510 --> 00:23:01.070 align:middle line:84%
it actually loads
libraries and loads

00:23:01.070 --> 00:23:06.510 align:middle line:84%
the data set of stock prices
that was just downloaded

00:23:06.510 --> 00:23:08.570 align:middle line:90%
in the other program.

00:23:08.570 --> 00:23:12.830 align:middle line:90%
And it loads the data in.

00:23:12.830 --> 00:23:20.970 align:middle line:84%
Then, in processing
the data, I--

00:23:20.970 --> 00:23:31.040 align:middle line:84%
consider investing a portfolio
by equally weighting allocations

00:23:31.040 --> 00:23:34.460 align:middle line:84%
to different stocks
in the S&P 500 index.

00:23:34.460 --> 00:23:40.460 align:middle line:84%
And so, if one does this,
these simple R commands create

00:23:40.460 --> 00:23:47.210 align:middle line:84%
the value of such a
portfolio, where, in fact,

00:23:47.210 --> 00:23:52.220 align:middle line:84%
as indicated here,
it's investing $1,000

00:23:52.220 --> 00:23:56.180 align:middle line:90%
at the beginning of 2019.

00:23:56.180 --> 00:24:01.580 align:middle line:84%
And as an alternative to just
equally weighting across the S&P

00:24:01.580 --> 00:24:08.990 align:middle line:84%
500 stocks, I looked at
the Amazon, Apple, NVIDIA--

00:24:08.990 --> 00:24:10.680 align:middle line:90%
or no, Netflix and Google.

00:24:10.680 --> 00:24:12.530 align:middle line:84%
These were actually
the really hot stocks

00:24:12.530 --> 00:24:14.440 align:middle line:90%
about four or five years ago.

00:24:14.440 --> 00:24:19.300 align:middle line:84%
And basically, look at equal
weighting of all these.

00:24:19.300 --> 00:24:24.580 align:middle line:84%
And so here's how
the investment, equal

00:24:24.580 --> 00:24:26.800 align:middle line:84%
weighted at the
beginning of the period,

00:24:26.800 --> 00:24:28.790 align:middle line:90%
resulted towards the end.

00:24:28.790 --> 00:24:33.160 align:middle line:84%
So there are some interesting
features one can see.

00:24:33.160 --> 00:24:39.230 align:middle line:84%
If one happened to be good at
identifying stocks to invest in,

00:24:39.230 --> 00:24:45.010 align:middle line:84%
you can do much better
than the average S&P 500.

00:24:45.010 --> 00:24:49.210 align:middle line:84%
There can be greater
risk by investing

00:24:49.210 --> 00:24:52.060 align:middle line:84%
in a smaller number of
stocks because you're not

00:24:52.060 --> 00:24:54.040 align:middle line:90%
so diversified.

00:24:54.040 --> 00:25:03.070 align:middle line:84%
And so you can see how
the red graph of values

00:25:03.070 --> 00:25:08.630 align:middle line:84%
decreases rather dramatically at
some points in the time period.

00:25:08.630 --> 00:25:15.840 align:middle line:84%
And if one looks at what
happened to the $250 invested

00:25:15.840 --> 00:25:18.960 align:middle line:84%
in each of those
four stocks, this

00:25:18.960 --> 00:25:21.832 align:middle line:84%
is what happened
over this, whatever

00:25:21.832 --> 00:25:25.390 align:middle line:84%
it is, five-year period,
or almost five-year period.

00:25:25.390 --> 00:25:29.650 align:middle line:84%
And this raises some
interesting issues for us,

00:25:29.650 --> 00:25:37.590 align:middle line:84%
which is, when considering
portfolio construction, maybe

00:25:37.590 --> 00:25:40.890 align:middle line:84%
equally weighting allocations
across some assets

00:25:40.890 --> 00:25:42.700 align:middle line:90%
is a good thing to start with.

00:25:42.700 --> 00:25:48.600 align:middle line:90%
But what happens over time?

00:25:48.600 --> 00:25:55.380 align:middle line:84%
Well, in this case, Apple
outgrew the other allocations

00:25:55.380 --> 00:25:56.560 align:middle line:90%
substantially.

00:25:56.560 --> 00:26:01.260 align:middle line:84%
And so the degree of
diversification in the portfolio

00:26:01.260 --> 00:26:04.590 align:middle line:84%
is getting lower because we
have a greater concentration

00:26:04.590 --> 00:26:05.980 align:middle line:90%
in that one stock.

00:26:05.980 --> 00:26:09.630 align:middle line:84%
So what we'd like
to do, perhaps,

00:26:09.630 --> 00:26:15.590 align:middle line:90%
is to rebalance the portfolio.

00:26:15.590 --> 00:26:21.590 align:middle line:84%
And different approaches
to rebalancing portfolios

00:26:21.590 --> 00:26:23.790 align:middle line:90%
can be considered.

00:26:23.790 --> 00:26:31.430 align:middle line:84%
Now, if we were to
consider rebalancing

00:26:31.430 --> 00:26:40.610 align:middle line:84%
every day the investments in
these four stocks, then would

00:26:40.610 --> 00:26:45.360 align:middle line:84%
you think that's a really good
way to rebalance, to do it very,

00:26:45.360 --> 00:26:46.760 align:middle line:90%
very frequently?

00:26:46.760 --> 00:26:48.355 align:middle line:90%
And why or why not?

00:26:48.355 --> 00:26:51.290 align:middle line:90%


00:26:51.290 --> 00:26:51.830 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:26:51.830 --> 00:26:53.663 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Well, if you
do it too frequently,

00:26:53.663 --> 00:26:57.590 align:middle line:84%
then variances like
2022 can affect it.

00:26:57.590 --> 00:27:01.280 align:middle line:84%
It won't be very good
for the big picture.

00:27:01.280 --> 00:27:02.540 align:middle line:90%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: Yeah.

00:27:02.540 --> 00:27:06.080 align:middle line:84%
If you rebalance too
quickly, then you're

00:27:06.080 --> 00:27:12.790 align:middle line:84%
taking funds away from winners
and giving those to losers.

00:27:12.790 --> 00:27:17.230 align:middle line:84%
And so you're
equalizing allocations

00:27:17.230 --> 00:27:22.540 align:middle line:84%
in a way that doesn't benefit
from short-term trends

00:27:22.540 --> 00:27:25.370 align:middle line:84%
or strengths in
different assets.

00:27:25.370 --> 00:27:33.520 align:middle line:84%
So that extreme is probably
not something you want to do.

00:27:33.520 --> 00:27:35.920 align:middle line:84%
One thing that's
rather interesting

00:27:35.920 --> 00:27:40.940 align:middle line:84%
is just how, if you do
not do any rebalancing,

00:27:40.940 --> 00:27:47.900 align:middle line:84%
then the portfolios become
rather undiversified.

00:27:47.900 --> 00:27:52.960 align:middle line:84%
And so the risk level is
actually probably increasing

00:27:52.960 --> 00:27:54.260 align:middle line:90%
over time here.

00:27:54.260 --> 00:27:57.850 align:middle line:84%
So one way of
rebalancing portfolios

00:27:57.850 --> 00:28:01.490 align:middle line:84%
is trying to maintain a certain
risk level for the portfolio.

00:28:01.490 --> 00:28:08.490 align:middle line:84%
And such an objective would
require periodic rebalancing.

00:28:08.490 --> 00:28:10.830 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:28:10.830 --> 00:28:26.530 align:middle line:84%
Well, let's move on, then, to
the topic of probability theory.

00:28:26.530 --> 00:28:31.770 align:middle line:84%
And so what I want to do in
this note is to just give you

00:28:31.770 --> 00:28:35.040 align:middle line:90%
a quick overview--

00:28:35.040 --> 00:28:38.160 align:middle line:84%
basically, a review
of probability

00:28:38.160 --> 00:28:41.550 align:middle line:84%
concepts that you probably
are very familiar with.

00:28:41.550 --> 00:28:47.610 align:middle line:84%
And so, with
probability theory, it's

00:28:47.610 --> 00:28:54.810 align:middle line:84%
customary to distinguish between
discrete-valued random variables

00:28:54.810 --> 00:28:57.070 align:middle line:90%
and continuous ones.

00:28:57.070 --> 00:29:00.720 align:middle line:84%
And so, with discrete-valued
random variables,

00:29:00.720 --> 00:29:04.080 align:middle line:84%
those can be just
events occurring or not,

00:29:04.080 --> 00:29:08.960 align:middle line:84%
like a default of a
counterparty, or maybe

00:29:08.960 --> 00:29:11.120 align:middle line:84%
the Federal Open
Market Committee

00:29:11.120 --> 00:29:14.003 align:middle line:90%
deciding on a Fed funds rate.

00:29:14.003 --> 00:29:18.410 align:middle line:90%


00:29:18.410 --> 00:29:25.350 align:middle line:84%
In thinking of individual stocks
and their dynamics intraday,

00:29:25.350 --> 00:29:28.940 align:middle line:84%
one can think of different
orders for a stock

00:29:28.940 --> 00:29:33.860 align:middle line:84%
to buy or sell
coming in at random

00:29:33.860 --> 00:29:36.540 align:middle line:84%
and wondering what side
those would be on--

00:29:36.540 --> 00:29:42.050 align:middle line:84%
also, perhaps, the sheer size
of the next market order.

00:29:42.050 --> 00:29:44.630 align:middle line:90%


00:29:44.630 --> 00:29:49.820 align:middle line:84%
Now, it used to be that these
were always discrete outcomes.

00:29:49.820 --> 00:29:51.950 align:middle line:84%
Like, share size, it
turns out you can actually

00:29:51.950 --> 00:29:55.860 align:middle line:84%
now invest fractional
shares in different stocks.

00:29:55.860 --> 00:30:02.150 align:middle line:84%
So the market's become
more rich in that way.

00:30:02.150 --> 00:30:05.500 align:middle line:84%
But, importantly, we
do want to distinguish

00:30:05.500 --> 00:30:10.390 align:middle line:84%
between discrete outcomes
that have probabilities

00:30:10.390 --> 00:30:14.930 align:middle line:84%
with respective outcomes, versus
continuous random variables,

00:30:14.930 --> 00:30:19.210 align:middle line:84%
where we're thinking
of values that

00:30:19.210 --> 00:30:23.570 align:middle line:84%
could take on any value
in some interval or range.

00:30:23.570 --> 00:30:28.870 align:middle line:84%
And so, in thinking about
the values of assets,

00:30:28.870 --> 00:30:33.620 align:middle line:84%
we can think of the value
being in a continuous range.

00:30:33.620 --> 00:30:41.560 align:middle line:84%
Also, timing of events is
an important random variable

00:30:41.560 --> 00:30:44.480 align:middle line:90%
that is continuous as well.

00:30:44.480 --> 00:30:48.880 align:middle line:84%
So one might consider,
what is the waiting

00:30:48.880 --> 00:30:55.810 align:middle line:84%
time to the next market
order for Apple stock?

00:30:55.810 --> 00:30:59.800 align:middle line:84%
Mixed variables
can arise where we

00:30:59.800 --> 00:31:05.400 align:middle line:84%
think of a stock having some
value, where it's generally

00:31:05.400 --> 00:31:09.960 align:middle line:84%
a positive value, but it could
go bankrupt, in which case

00:31:09.960 --> 00:31:12.870 align:middle line:84%
there would perhaps be
some discrete probability

00:31:12.870 --> 00:31:16.560 align:middle line:90%
of bankruptcy for a stock.

00:31:16.560 --> 00:31:23.670 align:middle line:84%
Now, with probability models, we
use probability mass functions

00:31:23.670 --> 00:31:28.380 align:middle line:84%
for discrete variables
and probability densities

00:31:28.380 --> 00:31:29.830 align:middle line:90%
for continuous.

00:31:29.830 --> 00:31:32.640 align:middle line:84%
And so we use
calculus to calculate

00:31:32.640 --> 00:31:37.590 align:middle line:84%
continuous probabilities
in different intervals.

00:31:37.590 --> 00:31:42.990 align:middle line:84%
And, importantly, the
cumulative distribution function

00:31:42.990 --> 00:31:48.870 align:middle line:90%
is a very important tool.

00:31:48.870 --> 00:31:52.260 align:middle line:90%
So if we write F sub x of--

00:31:52.260 --> 00:31:53.580 align:middle line:90%
let me write c--

00:31:53.580 --> 00:31:56.640 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the probability
that the random variable x

00:31:56.640 --> 00:32:01.380 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal to
c, as a function of c,

00:32:01.380 --> 00:32:13.170 align:middle line:84%
this value is going to be no
smaller than 0 and no larger

00:32:13.170 --> 00:32:14.530 align:middle line:90%
than 1.

00:32:14.530 --> 00:32:21.180 align:middle line:84%
And it will be monotone
increasing in c.

00:32:21.180 --> 00:32:25.200 align:middle line:84%
And distributions
will be uniquely

00:32:25.200 --> 00:32:29.490 align:middle line:84%
specified by specifying
their cumulative distribution

00:32:29.490 --> 00:32:30.520 align:middle line:90%
function.

00:32:30.520 --> 00:32:34.817 align:middle line:84%
So these are very
simple concepts.

00:32:34.817 --> 00:32:38.050 align:middle line:90%


00:32:38.050 --> 00:32:39.770 align:middle line:84%
There are expectations
in moments.

00:32:39.770 --> 00:32:43.470 align:middle line:90%


00:32:43.470 --> 00:32:47.966 align:middle line:84%
One just-- I'll just
call this an anecdote--

00:32:47.966 --> 00:32:50.820 align:middle line:90%


00:32:50.820 --> 00:32:57.800 align:middle line:84%
if x is continuous
random variable,

00:32:57.800 --> 00:33:06.620 align:middle line:84%
and we define y equal to f of x,
so we consider the probability

00:33:06.620 --> 00:33:13.220 align:middle line:84%
integral transform of
the random variable x,

00:33:13.220 --> 00:33:15.413 align:middle line:90%
what is the distribution of y?

00:33:15.413 --> 00:33:36.050 align:middle line:90%


00:33:36.050 --> 00:33:36.900 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:33:36.900 --> 00:33:37.650 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Uniform.

00:33:37.650 --> 00:33:40.032 align:middle line:84%
PETER KEMPTHORNE:
Yeah, it's uniform.

00:33:40.032 --> 00:33:41.570 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: On 0.

00:33:41.570 --> 00:33:43.760 align:middle line:90%
It's uniform on 0.

00:33:43.760 --> 00:33:45.650 align:middle line:84%
PETER KEMPTHORNE:
So what's really

00:33:45.650 --> 00:33:51.530 align:middle line:84%
neat is that a
monotone transform

00:33:51.530 --> 00:33:53.690 align:middle line:84%
of the random
variable x, whatever

00:33:53.690 --> 00:33:57.190 align:middle line:84%
x is, so long as we know
what the distribution is,

00:33:57.190 --> 00:33:59.720 align:middle line:90%
transforms that to a uniform.

00:33:59.720 --> 00:34:04.330 align:middle line:84%
And so this is true
regardless of what F

00:34:04.330 --> 00:34:07.150 align:middle line:90%
is, so long as F is continuous.

00:34:07.150 --> 00:34:12.683 align:middle line:84%
And that property can turn
out to be very, very useful.

00:34:12.683 --> 00:34:15.340 align:middle line:90%


00:34:15.340 --> 00:34:20.320 align:middle line:84%
One issue could be, oh, suppose
you have a random sample of data

00:34:20.320 --> 00:34:22.600 align:middle line:84%
from some distribution,
or you think

00:34:22.600 --> 00:34:24.550 align:middle line:90%
it's from some distribution.

00:34:24.550 --> 00:34:28.580 align:middle line:84%
Is the sample consistent with
that distribution or not?

00:34:28.580 --> 00:34:32.080 align:middle line:84%
Well, if you did this
probability integral transform,

00:34:32.080 --> 00:34:37.360 align:middle line:84%
it should lead to a uniform
distribution, which may or may

00:34:37.360 --> 00:34:39.409 align:middle line:90%
not be realized.

00:34:39.409 --> 00:34:43.969 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, that turns
out to be quite useful.

00:34:43.969 --> 00:34:51.040 align:middle line:84%
Now, we have expectations and
moments of random variables.

00:34:51.040 --> 00:34:54.280 align:middle line:84%
Basically, the mean
of random variables,

00:34:54.280 --> 00:34:56.530 align:middle line:84%
the probability-weighted
outcome,

00:34:56.530 --> 00:35:00.610 align:middle line:84%
we can generalize the
mean to looking at,

00:35:00.610 --> 00:35:03.660 align:middle line:84%
which is the first
moment, to consider

00:35:03.660 --> 00:35:08.250 align:middle line:84%
the probability-weighted
average of the k-th power, where

00:35:08.250 --> 00:35:09.820 align:middle line:90%
k is an integer.

00:35:09.820 --> 00:35:14.190 align:middle line:84%
And the variance
of random variables

00:35:14.190 --> 00:35:17.160 align:middle line:84%
is a useful measure
of uncertainty

00:35:17.160 --> 00:35:19.720 align:middle line:90%
about the mean value.

00:35:19.720 --> 00:35:25.410 align:middle line:84%
And there's a nice formula
for the variance, which

00:35:25.410 --> 00:35:30.240 align:middle line:84%
is the squared distance
from the mean in terms

00:35:30.240 --> 00:35:34.510 align:middle line:84%
of the average squared minus
the square of the average.

00:35:34.510 --> 00:35:39.210 align:middle line:84%
So these are useful
formulas that

00:35:39.210 --> 00:35:42.580 align:middle line:90%
may be useful at certain times.

00:35:42.580 --> 00:35:46.020 align:middle line:84%
Now in terms of
measuring uncertainty

00:35:46.020 --> 00:35:51.980 align:middle line:84%
about outcomes, instead
of the variance,

00:35:51.980 --> 00:35:53.870 align:middle line:84%
taking the square
root of the variance

00:35:53.870 --> 00:36:01.080 align:middle line:84%
is useful because its scale is
the same as the original units.

00:36:01.080 --> 00:36:05.060 align:middle line:84%
Most people don't think
in terms of squared units

00:36:05.060 --> 00:36:07.670 align:middle line:90%
with any problem.

00:36:07.670 --> 00:36:12.060 align:middle line:84%
And so, instead of dealing
with the variance directly,

00:36:12.060 --> 00:36:15.080 align:middle line:84%
we'll commonly take
the square root

00:36:15.080 --> 00:36:20.010 align:middle line:84%
of that to have the same units
as our original variable, x.

00:36:20.010 --> 00:36:26.420 align:middle line:84%
Now, with the
skewness and kurtosis,

00:36:26.420 --> 00:36:33.410 align:middle line:84%
we're looking at measures of
the shape of the distribution.

00:36:33.410 --> 00:36:38.000 align:middle line:84%
And with skewness, as
you might anticipate,

00:36:38.000 --> 00:36:42.870 align:middle line:84%
that detects asymmetry
in a distribution.

00:36:42.870 --> 00:36:47.330 align:middle line:84%
And so, something that's
nice to think about

00:36:47.330 --> 00:36:55.180 align:middle line:84%
is, if we have a random variable
x, where the expected value of x

00:36:55.180 --> 00:37:04.510 align:middle line:84%
is equal to mu, and the square
root of the variance is sigma,

00:37:04.510 --> 00:37:12.680 align:middle line:84%
if we take z equal to
x minus mu over sigma,

00:37:12.680 --> 00:37:18.640 align:middle line:84%
this is a standardization
of the random variable.

00:37:18.640 --> 00:37:23.090 align:middle line:84%
The expected value
of z will equal 0.

00:37:23.090 --> 00:37:29.530 align:middle line:84%
And the variance
of z will equal 1,

00:37:29.530 --> 00:37:32.180 align:middle line:84%
as will the square
root of the variance.

00:37:32.180 --> 00:37:36.980 align:middle line:84%
And for the skewness,
which I use--

00:37:36.980 --> 00:37:38.680 align:middle line:90%
I guess it's a gamma--

00:37:38.680 --> 00:37:43.430 align:middle line:84%
gamma is equal to the
expected value of z cubed.

00:37:43.430 --> 00:37:51.090 align:middle line:84%
And the kurtosis is equal to the
expected value of z to the 4th.

00:37:51.090 --> 00:37:59.580 align:middle line:84%
So the skewness and kurtosis
parameters of a distribution

00:37:59.580 --> 00:38:01.480 align:middle line:90%
can measure asymmetry.

00:38:01.480 --> 00:38:05.130 align:middle line:84%
At least, if the cubed
value has more weight

00:38:05.130 --> 00:38:08.760 align:middle line:84%
on the positive end
than the negative end,

00:38:08.760 --> 00:38:11.740 align:middle line:90%
it'll be skewed to the right.

00:38:11.740 --> 00:38:18.520 align:middle line:84%
And with the kappa, we're
looking at z to the 4th.

00:38:18.520 --> 00:38:23.140 align:middle line:84%
So we're looking at, basically,
how heavy are the tails?

00:38:23.140 --> 00:38:32.040 align:middle line:84%
And for a Gaussian
distribution, kappa

00:38:32.040 --> 00:38:39.630 align:middle line:84%
is equal to 3 for a normal,
0, 1, or any normal.

00:38:39.630 --> 00:38:44.500 align:middle line:90%
And gamma is equal to 0.

00:38:44.500 --> 00:38:47.330 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, so these
other parameters

00:38:47.330 --> 00:38:51.020 align:middle line:84%
are useful for
characterizing distributions

00:38:51.020 --> 00:38:53.930 align:middle line:90%
that are not Gaussian--

00:38:53.930 --> 00:38:55.640 align:middle line:84%
non-Gaussian in
terms of, perhaps,

00:38:55.640 --> 00:38:57.940 align:middle line:90%
skewness or heavier tails.

00:38:57.940 --> 00:39:01.220 align:middle line:90%


00:39:01.220 --> 00:39:01.830 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:39:01.830 --> 00:39:08.020 align:middle line:84%
Well, let's just review
Gaussian distributions.

00:39:08.020 --> 00:39:10.790 align:middle line:90%


00:39:10.790 --> 00:39:14.840 align:middle line:84%
I hope this formula for
the density of a Gaussian

00:39:14.840 --> 00:39:18.080 align:middle line:84%
is very familiar
to everyone here.

00:39:18.080 --> 00:39:29.510 align:middle line:84%
We basically have, the density
function is equal to 1 over root

00:39:29.510 --> 00:39:38.660 align:middle line:84%
2 pi sigma e to the minus 1/2
x minus mu over sigma squared.

00:39:38.660 --> 00:39:43.620 align:middle line:90%
And so the mean is mu.

00:39:43.620 --> 00:39:45.760 align:middle line:90%
The variance is sigma squared.

00:39:45.760 --> 00:39:49.540 align:middle line:84%
And the density
function is symmetrical,

00:39:49.540 --> 00:39:52.880 align:middle line:90%
with a maximum at the mean, mu.

00:39:52.880 --> 00:39:57.880 align:middle line:84%
And the points of
inflection are actually

00:39:57.880 --> 00:39:59.750 align:middle line:90%
at plus and minus sigma.

00:39:59.750 --> 00:40:09.370 align:middle line:84%
So if we think of just trying to
draw a normal density function,

00:40:09.370 --> 00:40:13.430 align:middle line:90%
then the peak is at the mean.

00:40:13.430 --> 00:40:19.780 align:middle line:84%
And the point of inflection
where the second derivative is

00:40:19.780 --> 00:40:24.070 align:middle line:84%
0, it goes from negative
second derivative

00:40:24.070 --> 00:40:26.330 align:middle line:90%
to a positive second derivative.

00:40:26.330 --> 00:40:31.396 align:middle line:84%
That distance is
sigma, symmetrically.

00:40:31.396 --> 00:40:35.260 align:middle line:90%


00:40:35.260 --> 00:40:36.700 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:40:36.700 --> 00:40:41.980 align:middle line:84%
So let's take a look at
the normal bell curve.

00:40:41.980 --> 00:40:46.190 align:middle line:84%
68% lies within 1
standard deviation.

00:40:46.190 --> 00:40:49.450 align:middle line:90%
So this is whatever--

00:40:49.450 --> 00:40:52.990 align:middle line:84%
AP Statistics, you
learn these facts.

00:40:52.990 --> 00:40:55.550 align:middle line:84%
If we go to 2 standard
deviations from the mean,

00:40:55.550 --> 00:40:57.380 align:middle line:90%
that's 95%.

00:40:57.380 --> 00:41:01.340 align:middle line:84%
And 3 standard
deviations is 99.7%.

00:41:01.340 --> 00:41:05.560 align:middle line:90%
So these models are--

00:41:05.560 --> 00:41:08.500 align:middle line:84%
normal models have
these properties

00:41:08.500 --> 00:41:12.070 align:middle line:84%
of how likely different
variations are

00:41:12.070 --> 00:41:16.150 align:middle line:90%
in standard deviation units.

00:41:16.150 --> 00:41:22.300 align:middle line:84%
Well, in finance, we'd
like to exploit, perhaps,

00:41:22.300 --> 00:41:26.290 align:middle line:84%
Gaussian distributions
for modeling prices.

00:41:26.290 --> 00:41:34.090 align:middle line:84%
And with a normal density
curve, it actually

00:41:34.090 --> 00:41:38.020 align:middle line:84%
applies to x values
that can be arbitrarily

00:41:38.020 --> 00:41:40.580 align:middle line:90%
large, negative or positive.

00:41:40.580 --> 00:41:46.150 align:middle line:84%
It's an unbounded distribution
in terms of potential outcomes.

00:41:46.150 --> 00:41:51.330 align:middle line:84%
And so one way of dealing
with that with, say, prices

00:41:51.330 --> 00:41:57.390 align:middle line:84%
is to consider taking
the log of prices

00:41:57.390 --> 00:42:01.080 align:middle line:84%
and thinking of that being
a normal distribution.

00:42:01.080 --> 00:42:05.820 align:middle line:84%
And so that
construction corresponds

00:42:05.820 --> 00:42:09.040 align:middle line:90%
to a lognormal distribution.

00:42:09.040 --> 00:42:14.580 align:middle line:84%
And so if we have a random
variable x that's normal,

00:42:14.580 --> 00:42:18.880 align:middle line:84%
and then we take the
exponential of that normal,

00:42:18.880 --> 00:42:23.850 align:middle line:84%
then we say that the y
variable is lognormal,

00:42:23.850 --> 00:42:27.180 align:middle line:84%
with the parameters mu and
sigma squared corresponding

00:42:27.180 --> 00:42:28.920 align:middle line:90%
to the log scale.

00:42:28.920 --> 00:42:35.940 align:middle line:84%
Now, what will be
very interesting

00:42:35.940 --> 00:42:38.310 align:middle line:84%
in subsequent
lectures is when we

00:42:38.310 --> 00:42:45.350 align:middle line:84%
talk about stochastic
processes that can be

00:42:45.350 --> 00:42:48.690 align:middle line:90%
applied in financial markets.

00:42:48.690 --> 00:42:51.980 align:middle line:84%
There's an important
stochastic process

00:42:51.980 --> 00:42:57.020 align:middle line:84%
called Brownian motion,
which many of you

00:42:57.020 --> 00:42:59.810 align:middle line:84%
probably have some
familiarity with.

00:42:59.810 --> 00:43:03.720 align:middle line:84%
And with a Brownian
motion process,

00:43:03.720 --> 00:43:11.000 align:middle line:84%
the increments of the
process over time periods

00:43:11.000 --> 00:43:16.590 align:middle line:84%
has a distribution that's
normally distributed.

00:43:16.590 --> 00:43:22.370 align:middle line:84%
And as the length of the
increment of time increases,

00:43:22.370 --> 00:43:28.560 align:middle line:84%
both the mean and variance
of the outcome changes.

00:43:28.560 --> 00:43:31.340 align:middle line:84%
And so this Brownian
motion process

00:43:31.340 --> 00:43:35.000 align:middle line:84%
is a very, very
interesting process

00:43:35.000 --> 00:43:40.100 align:middle line:84%
that is intimately related
to Gaussian distribution.

00:43:40.100 --> 00:43:44.210 align:middle line:84%
So we'll get there
in a few lectures.

00:43:44.210 --> 00:43:51.250 align:middle line:84%
Now, one of the useful tools
we have in probability theory

00:43:51.250 --> 00:43:58.390 align:middle line:84%
is being able to derive the
distributions of transformations

00:43:58.390 --> 00:44:00.200 align:middle line:90%
of random variables.

00:44:00.200 --> 00:44:04.210 align:middle line:84%
So with the lognormal, we
have a normal distribution.

00:44:04.210 --> 00:44:08.330 align:middle line:84%
We maybe take the exponential
of that normal outcome.

00:44:08.330 --> 00:44:10.630 align:middle line:84%
And we'd like to be
able to write down

00:44:10.630 --> 00:44:15.590 align:middle line:84%
the density of that distribution
and compute different features.

00:44:15.590 --> 00:44:23.680 align:middle line:84%
Well, we can think of, say,
a random variable x being

00:44:23.680 --> 00:44:27.370 align:middle line:84%
transformed to y
with a function g.

00:44:27.370 --> 00:44:33.490 align:middle line:84%
And then we can consider
a function h, which

00:44:33.490 --> 00:44:36.840 align:middle line:90%
is the inverse function of g.

00:44:36.840 --> 00:44:43.090 align:middle line:84%
And as examples,
if g is e to the x,

00:44:43.090 --> 00:44:47.160 align:middle line:90%
then h is the log function.

00:44:47.160 --> 00:44:55.950 align:middle line:84%
And with change of variables,
if we have the CDF of x, then

00:44:55.950 --> 00:45:02.310 align:middle line:84%
the CDF of y is
equal to the CDF of x

00:45:02.310 --> 00:45:07.000 align:middle line:84%
evaluated at the inverse
of y on the x scale.

00:45:07.000 --> 00:45:13.330 align:middle line:84%
So this equation here is
a simple logical statement

00:45:13.330 --> 00:45:17.920 align:middle line:84%
that everyone here, I
think, would agree is valid.

00:45:17.920 --> 00:45:23.250 align:middle line:84%
And so, if this function
relating the distribution

00:45:23.250 --> 00:45:30.810 align:middle line:84%
functions is true, if we take
the derivative of the CDF

00:45:30.810 --> 00:45:36.380 align:middle line:84%
function of y, we
get the density.

00:45:36.380 --> 00:45:39.560 align:middle line:84%
And then we just have
to apply the chain rule

00:45:39.560 --> 00:45:45.080 align:middle line:84%
on the right-hand
side, the density of x

00:45:45.080 --> 00:45:47.430 align:middle line:90%
evaluated at h of y.

00:45:47.430 --> 00:45:52.530 align:middle line:84%
But then we also need to have
the chain rule for h in there.

00:45:52.530 --> 00:46:04.530 align:middle line:84%
So with this formula,
it's all familiar.

00:46:04.530 --> 00:46:11.810 align:middle line:84%
But if we have f of x is
equal to the integral minus

00:46:11.810 --> 00:46:21.620 align:middle line:84%
infinity to x of f of t
dt, then the derivative

00:46:21.620 --> 00:46:27.380 align:middle line:84%
of f evaluated at x turns
out to be equal to f

00:46:27.380 --> 00:46:30.685 align:middle line:90%
of t evaluated at t equal to x.

00:46:30.685 --> 00:46:33.190 align:middle line:90%


00:46:33.190 --> 00:46:37.240 align:middle line:84%
So the cumulative
integral has a derivative

00:46:37.240 --> 00:46:39.470 align:middle line:84%
equal to the argument
of the integral.

00:46:39.470 --> 00:46:41.750 align:middle line:90%
So this is easy.

00:46:41.750 --> 00:46:44.920 align:middle line:84%
And we can use this to
get the density function

00:46:44.920 --> 00:46:48.580 align:middle line:90%
for a lognormal distribution.

00:46:48.580 --> 00:46:56.180 align:middle line:84%
And here's the density of
the lognormal distribution.

00:46:56.180 --> 00:47:00.380 align:middle line:84%
It has the same form as
the Gaussian density,

00:47:00.380 --> 00:47:05.110 align:middle line:90%
plugging in log y for x.

00:47:05.110 --> 00:47:11.050 align:middle line:84%
But then we need to multiply
that by the chain rules,

00:47:11.050 --> 00:47:18.175 align:middle line:84%
or the derivative of h, with
respect to y, which is 1 over y.

00:47:18.175 --> 00:47:21.460 align:middle line:84%
So here's the
lognormal distribution.

00:47:21.460 --> 00:47:28.090 align:middle line:84%
And as an example, I chose
a mean log value of 0.2

00:47:28.090 --> 00:47:33.540 align:middle line:84%
and a SD on the
log scale of 0.4.

00:47:33.540 --> 00:47:38.943 align:middle line:84%
And as you work with different
distributions, you may have--

00:47:38.943 --> 00:47:43.320 align:middle line:90%


00:47:43.320 --> 00:47:45.150 align:middle line:84%
not suggestions,
but you may find

00:47:45.150 --> 00:47:49.840 align:middle line:84%
it useful to consider
specific parameter values.

00:47:49.840 --> 00:47:56.880 align:middle line:84%
Here, I'm thinking
that if one has a--

00:47:56.880 --> 00:48:02.130 align:middle line:84%
if we think of, say,
x of 0 equaling 1

00:48:02.130 --> 00:48:09.360 align:middle line:84%
and x of 1 being
distributed as a lognormal,

00:48:09.360 --> 00:48:14.350 align:middle line:84%
with mu equal to 0.2
and sigma equal to 0.4,

00:48:14.350 --> 00:48:20.910 align:middle line:84%
then this lognormal
distribution can

00:48:20.910 --> 00:48:28.740 align:middle line:84%
correspond to a one-year
period where, on the log scale,

00:48:28.740 --> 00:48:35.840 align:middle line:84%
we expect a mean change on
the log scale to be 0.2,

00:48:35.840 --> 00:48:40.350 align:middle line:84%
and with standard
deviation of 0.4.

00:48:40.350 --> 00:48:47.000 align:middle line:84%
So this would potentially
be relevant for an asset

00:48:47.000 --> 00:48:49.550 align:middle line:84%
and looking at its
percentage change

00:48:49.550 --> 00:48:53.780 align:middle line:84%
on the log scale over 1
year, with an annualized

00:48:53.780 --> 00:48:56.930 align:middle line:84%
return of about
20% and annualized

00:48:56.930 --> 00:48:58.910 align:middle line:90%
standard deviation of 0.4.

00:48:58.910 --> 00:49:04.040 align:middle line:84%
Well, if we draw vertical
lines in this distribution

00:49:04.040 --> 00:49:08.390 align:middle line:84%
space, or sample space,
of the lognormal--

00:49:08.390 --> 00:49:12.380 align:middle line:84%
here, I've just drawn
symmetrical percentiles--

00:49:12.380 --> 00:49:18.020 align:middle line:84%
25th and 75th percentiles,
and then the 5th and 95th

00:49:18.020 --> 00:49:19.770 align:middle line:90%
percentiles in red.

00:49:19.770 --> 00:49:24.170 align:middle line:84%
And it's obvious that
it's asymmetrical.

00:49:24.170 --> 00:49:34.720 align:middle line:84%
And what's useful, actually,
with different distributions

00:49:34.720 --> 00:49:38.710 align:middle line:84%
is being able to calculate
quantities of interest

00:49:38.710 --> 00:49:39.680 align:middle line:90%
in finance.

00:49:39.680 --> 00:49:48.130 align:middle line:84%
And so here's a nice example of
a financial computation, where

00:49:48.130 --> 00:49:54.880 align:middle line:84%
we consider a call option to buy
an asset at some terminal time,

00:49:54.880 --> 00:50:01.580 align:middle line:84%
capital T. And the strike price
is going to be at price K.

00:50:01.580 --> 00:50:09.940 align:middle line:84%
And so if we think of X being
the random variable of what

00:50:09.940 --> 00:50:14.410 align:middle line:84%
the time T price
is, then the payoff

00:50:14.410 --> 00:50:22.360 align:middle line:84%
is going to be equal to X minus
K, the value minus the strike

00:50:22.360 --> 00:50:28.490 align:middle line:84%
price, if that's positive, or
it won't be worth anything.

00:50:28.490 --> 00:50:33.713 align:middle line:90%
So if we have, at time T--

00:50:33.713 --> 00:50:36.460 align:middle line:90%


00:50:36.460 --> 00:50:40.600 align:middle line:84%
if we were to draw here
as a function, P sub T--

00:50:40.600 --> 00:50:43.630 align:middle line:90%
or sorry, X, I guess--

00:50:43.630 --> 00:50:47.530 align:middle line:84%
and there's a strike
price here, then

00:50:47.530 --> 00:50:50.230 align:middle line:84%
we would have the
0 value up to K.

00:50:50.230 --> 00:50:58.400 align:middle line:84%
And then we'd have X minus K
be giving this option payoff.

00:50:58.400 --> 00:51:05.050 align:middle line:84%
Now, you'll hear Vasiliy and
Jake talk about the hockey stick

00:51:05.050 --> 00:51:08.150 align:middle line:90%
payoff function of options.

00:51:08.150 --> 00:51:11.480 align:middle line:84%
And so this is what
that comes from.

00:51:11.480 --> 00:51:18.520 align:middle line:84%
Now, what's interesting to
know is this theorem here

00:51:18.520 --> 00:51:24.360 align:middle line:84%
says that if we have a
density function little f,

00:51:24.360 --> 00:51:30.960 align:middle line:84%
with CDF capital F, for the
outcome at time capital T,

00:51:30.960 --> 00:51:37.350 align:middle line:84%
then the expected
value of this payoff

00:51:37.350 --> 00:51:41.820 align:middle line:84%
is equal to this
integral from the strike

00:51:41.820 --> 00:51:45.990 align:middle line:90%
to infinity of 1 minus F of x.

00:51:45.990 --> 00:51:55.050 align:middle line:84%
And the proof of this
is done by considering

00:51:55.050 --> 00:52:02.940 align:middle line:84%
a finite upper limit, M, capital
M, and then integrating by parts

00:52:02.940 --> 00:52:05.080 align:middle line:90%
and getting a formula here.

00:52:05.080 --> 00:52:11.040 align:middle line:84%
So it's an interesting
result that if we

00:52:11.040 --> 00:52:22.260 align:middle line:84%
know the terminal time T
random price of an asset,

00:52:22.260 --> 00:52:28.220 align:middle line:84%
then a call option on
that terminal value

00:52:28.220 --> 00:52:32.000 align:middle line:90%
will have this expected value.

00:52:32.000 --> 00:52:39.470 align:middle line:84%
So those of you who actually
have some advanced probability

00:52:39.470 --> 00:52:52.160 align:middle line:84%
coursework may notice that when
you are looking at the expected

00:52:52.160 --> 00:52:55.190 align:middle line:84%
value of a positive
random variable,

00:52:55.190 --> 00:52:58.550 align:middle line:84%
you can sometimes express
that as an integral

00:52:58.550 --> 00:53:06.600 align:middle line:84%
of the complementary probability
of exceeding that value.

00:53:06.600 --> 00:53:11.240 align:middle line:84%
And so that result ends
up being applicable

00:53:11.240 --> 00:53:14.090 align:middle line:90%
here in a very nice way.

00:53:14.090 --> 00:53:17.990 align:middle line:84%
Now, with a normal
distribution for X,

00:53:17.990 --> 00:53:22.190 align:middle line:84%
we get this formula coming
out for that call option.

00:53:22.190 --> 00:53:27.410 align:middle line:84%
And if we have a
lognormal, we get

00:53:27.410 --> 00:53:31.810 align:middle line:84%
this different result. So
knowing the distribution

00:53:31.810 --> 00:53:36.910 align:middle line:84%
affects what the
option price is.

00:53:36.910 --> 00:53:42.370 align:middle line:84%
Now, in probability
theory, we make

00:53:42.370 --> 00:53:45.400 align:middle line:84%
use of moment-generating
functions, which,

00:53:45.400 --> 00:53:48.610 align:middle line:84%
I guess, when I first learned
of these, I thought, well,

00:53:48.610 --> 00:53:53.000 align:middle line:84%
nice tool, but I
don't really care.

00:53:53.000 --> 00:53:56.390 align:middle line:84%
At least, it's a highly
technical tool to use.

00:53:56.390 --> 00:54:01.510 align:middle line:84%
However, it turns out these
are incredibly useful,

00:54:01.510 --> 00:54:03.410 align:middle line:90%
moment-generating functions.

00:54:03.410 --> 00:54:07.570 align:middle line:84%
And the definition of the
moment-generating function

00:54:07.570 --> 00:54:18.600 align:middle line:84%
is given by looking at just the
expected value of e to the tx.

00:54:18.600 --> 00:54:21.330 align:middle line:90%


00:54:21.330 --> 00:54:31.080 align:middle line:84%
And we can expand this argument
in a Taylor series, which

00:54:31.080 --> 00:54:37.840 align:middle line:84%
is done on the first line there,
and just take the expectation.

00:54:37.840 --> 00:54:46.350 align:middle line:84%
Now, something that we should
be questioning, perhaps,

00:54:46.350 --> 00:54:52.020 align:middle line:84%
is, do moment-generating
functions always exist?

00:54:52.020 --> 00:54:59.550 align:middle line:84%
And by that, I mean, do we get a
finite-valued function, mx of t,

00:54:59.550 --> 00:55:01.886 align:middle line:90%
for all random variables?

00:55:01.886 --> 00:55:16.560 align:middle line:90%


00:55:16.560 --> 00:55:17.060 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:55:17.060 --> 00:55:19.580 align:middle line:90%


00:55:19.580 --> 00:55:21.710 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: No?

00:55:21.710 --> 00:55:22.880 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

00:55:22.880 --> 00:55:24.120 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: No, that's fine.

00:55:24.120 --> 00:55:24.900 align:middle line:90%
No, that's fine.

00:55:24.900 --> 00:55:26.335 align:middle line:90%
That's fine.

00:55:26.335 --> 00:55:26.960 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:55:26.960 --> 00:55:28.850 align:middle line:90%
No, no, no, that's--

00:55:28.850 --> 00:55:29.600 align:middle line:90%
yeah.

00:55:29.600 --> 00:55:31.600 align:middle line:84%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: The
moment-generating function

00:55:31.600 --> 00:55:34.220 align:middle line:90%
doesn't have to exist.

00:55:34.220 --> 00:55:36.080 align:middle line:84%
And the moment-generating
function

00:55:36.080 --> 00:55:42.060 align:middle line:84%
won't exist if this exponential
function has too much weight.

00:55:42.060 --> 00:55:44.750 align:middle line:90%
So that integral is infinite.

00:55:44.750 --> 00:55:47.870 align:middle line:84%
And an example
where it is infinite

00:55:47.870 --> 00:55:52.160 align:middle line:90%
is a Cauchy distribution.

00:55:52.160 --> 00:55:57.440 align:middle line:84%
A Cauchy distribution doesn't
have finite mean or variance.

00:55:57.440 --> 00:56:01.520 align:middle line:90%
But what's interesting is that--

00:56:01.520 --> 00:56:05.000 align:middle line:84%
this is called the
moment-generating function.

00:56:05.000 --> 00:56:13.400 align:middle line:84%
There's a characteristic
function--

00:56:13.400 --> 00:56:19.010 align:middle line:84%
characteristic
function-- that turns out

00:56:19.010 --> 00:56:28.250 align:middle line:84%
to be equal to e to the
itx, where i here is

00:56:28.250 --> 00:56:32.090 align:middle line:90%
the square root of negative 1.

00:56:32.090 --> 00:56:37.910 align:middle line:84%
And if you know a little
bit about complex numbers,

00:56:37.910 --> 00:56:42.020 align:middle line:84%
this turns out to be
equal to the cosine of tx

00:56:42.020 --> 00:56:46.580 align:middle line:90%
plus i times the sine of tx.

00:56:46.580 --> 00:56:49.640 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out that these
characteristic functions

00:56:49.640 --> 00:56:55.290 align:middle line:84%
always exist and can be
used almost the same way

00:56:55.290 --> 00:56:59.190 align:middle line:84%
a moment-generating function
can be used when it exists.

00:56:59.190 --> 00:57:02.070 align:middle line:84%
So the fact that it
doesn't always exist

00:57:02.070 --> 00:57:04.730 align:middle line:84%
shouldn't worry us
because we can generalize

00:57:04.730 --> 00:57:06.990 align:middle line:90%
to characteristic functions.

00:57:06.990 --> 00:57:14.550 align:middle line:84%
But what's important is that
if we have two random variables

00:57:14.550 --> 00:57:19.050 align:middle line:84%
that have identical
moment-generating functions,

00:57:19.050 --> 00:57:25.200 align:middle line:84%
then those distributions
are equal for the two

00:57:25.200 --> 00:57:26.380 align:middle line:90%
random variables.

00:57:26.380 --> 00:57:28.290 align:middle line:84%
So we have identical
distributions

00:57:28.290 --> 00:57:31.120 align:middle line:84%
if their moment-generating
functions are equal.

00:57:31.120 --> 00:57:37.590 align:middle line:84%
And if we consider a
sequence of random variables,

00:57:37.590 --> 00:57:43.290 align:middle line:84%
and the moment-generating
functions that

00:57:43.290 --> 00:57:48.480 align:middle line:84%
can be thought of as a sequence
converges to a function that's

00:57:48.480 --> 00:57:51.220 align:middle line:84%
a moment-generating function
of some distribution,

00:57:51.220 --> 00:57:56.970 align:middle line:84%
then we have that the sequence
of random variables, Xs,

00:57:56.970 --> 00:57:59.970 align:middle line:84%
converges to that
distribution as well.

00:57:59.970 --> 00:58:03.990 align:middle line:84%
So these are nice
properties that

00:58:03.990 --> 00:58:10.190 align:middle line:84%
allow us to understand
limits of random variables

00:58:10.190 --> 00:58:14.510 align:middle line:84%
and what the distributions
are of limits.

00:58:14.510 --> 00:58:21.020 align:middle line:84%
Now, with moment-generating
functions,

00:58:21.020 --> 00:58:25.730 align:middle line:90%
this slide here discusses--

00:58:25.730 --> 00:58:28.820 align:middle line:84%
well, it introduces you to
the moment-generating function

00:58:28.820 --> 00:58:32.720 align:middle line:84%
for the normal distribution
with mean mu and variance

00:58:32.720 --> 00:58:34.130 align:middle line:90%
sigma squared.

00:58:34.130 --> 00:58:43.180 align:middle line:84%
And with the special case of mu
equals 0 and sigma equaling 1--

00:58:43.180 --> 00:58:46.980 align:middle line:90%


00:58:46.980 --> 00:58:53.030 align:middle line:84%
for a normal, 0, 1, if we look
at the expected value of e

00:58:53.030 --> 00:59:02.090 align:middle line:84%
to the tx, it's equal to the
integral of e to the tx times e

00:59:02.090 --> 00:59:10.550 align:middle line:84%
to the minus 1/2 x
squared over root 2 pi dx.

00:59:10.550 --> 00:59:21.200 align:middle line:84%
So this is simply the formula
for e to the tz, I guess.

00:59:21.200 --> 00:59:26.640 align:middle line:84%
Well, if we look at
this exponential here,

00:59:26.640 --> 00:59:32.113 align:middle line:84%
we can write this as e to
the minus 1/2 x minus--

00:59:32.113 --> 00:59:40.130 align:middle line:90%


00:59:40.130 --> 00:59:49.580 align:middle line:84%
let's see-- x minus
t over 2 squared.

00:59:49.580 --> 00:59:52.953 align:middle line:90%
That gives us x squared minus--

00:59:52.953 --> 00:59:57.560 align:middle line:90%


00:59:57.560 --> 00:59:59.935 align:middle line:90%
oh, no, just x minus t squared.

00:59:59.935 --> 01:00:05.080 align:middle line:90%


01:00:05.080 --> 01:00:06.160 align:middle line:90%
Is this right?

01:00:06.160 --> 01:00:09.220 align:middle line:90%


01:00:09.220 --> 01:00:23.470 align:middle line:84%
I basically want to take 1/2 x
squared plus tx and write this

01:00:23.470 --> 01:00:40.000 align:middle line:84%
as minus 1/2 of x minus t
squared minus t squared over 2.

01:00:40.000 --> 01:00:42.040 align:middle line:90%
Is that right?

01:00:42.040 --> 01:00:46.060 align:middle line:90%
I want a plus right there.

01:00:46.060 --> 01:00:51.850 align:middle line:84%
So this exponent
of the exponential

01:00:51.850 --> 01:00:56.600 align:middle line:84%
here can be rewritten as
this exponent times that.

01:00:56.600 --> 01:01:05.100 align:middle line:90%


01:01:05.100 --> 01:01:09.250 align:middle line:84%
So this is equal to the integral
from minus infinity to infinity,

01:01:09.250 --> 01:01:16.920 align:middle line:84%
1 over root 2 pi, e to the
minus 1/2, x minus t squared,

01:01:16.920 --> 01:01:23.250 align:middle line:84%
and then plus t
squared over 2 dx.

01:01:23.250 --> 01:01:34.930 align:middle line:84%
And so this exponential factor
with t squared can be taken out.

01:01:34.930 --> 01:01:39.180 align:middle line:84%
And this integral here is
simply going to equal 1.

01:01:39.180 --> 01:01:42.390 align:middle line:84%
So we are able to find the
moment-generating function

01:01:42.390 --> 01:01:47.040 align:middle line:84%
for the standard
normal very easily.

01:01:47.040 --> 01:01:49.840 align:middle line:84%
It's equal to e to
the 1/2 t squared.

01:01:49.840 --> 01:01:55.140 align:middle line:84%
Now, if we think about
transforming a random variable,

01:01:55.140 --> 01:02:01.220 align:middle line:84%
such as X, with a linear
function-- basically,

01:02:01.220 --> 01:02:05.570 align:middle line:84%
rescale X by sigma
and add a mu--

01:02:05.570 --> 01:02:07.820 align:middle line:84%
then the
moment-generating function

01:02:07.820 --> 01:02:14.040 align:middle line:84%
of that transformation of
X to Y can be written out.

01:02:14.040 --> 01:02:16.040 align:middle line:84%
And it's equal to
the exponential

01:02:16.040 --> 01:02:19.970 align:middle line:84%
of t mu times the
moment-generating function of X

01:02:19.970 --> 01:02:22.850 align:middle line:90%
evaluated at t sigma instead.

01:02:22.850 --> 01:02:29.070 align:middle line:84%
So knowing what the X
moment-generating function is,

01:02:29.070 --> 01:02:31.040 align:middle line:84%
we basically get this
function coming out

01:02:31.040 --> 01:02:36.510 align:middle line:84%
for the moment-generating
function of a normal.

01:02:36.510 --> 01:02:42.380 align:middle line:84%
Now, this
moment-generating function

01:02:42.380 --> 01:02:51.273 align:middle line:84%
is, in fact, the expected value
of a lognormal random variable.

01:02:51.273 --> 01:02:53.900 align:middle line:90%


01:02:53.900 --> 01:02:56.400 align:middle line:84%
At least, if we
set t equal to 1,

01:02:56.400 --> 01:03:01.210 align:middle line:84%
it's the expected
value of a lognormal.

01:03:01.210 --> 01:03:04.390 align:middle line:84%
And if we take t
equal to 2, it's

01:03:04.390 --> 01:03:09.170 align:middle line:84%
equal to the square of a
lognormal distribution.

01:03:09.170 --> 01:03:12.580 align:middle line:84%
So we can use the
moment-generating function

01:03:12.580 --> 01:03:14.380 align:middle line:84%
for the normal to
calculate moments

01:03:14.380 --> 01:03:16.010 align:middle line:90%
of a lognormal distribution.

01:03:16.010 --> 01:03:19.650 align:middle line:84%
And that'll be an exercise
in the next homework.

01:03:19.650 --> 01:03:22.390 align:middle line:90%


01:03:22.390 --> 01:03:22.890 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:03:22.890 --> 01:03:25.480 align:middle line:90%


01:03:25.480 --> 01:03:27.970 align:middle line:84%
All right, so linear
transformations--

01:03:27.970 --> 01:03:31.630 align:middle line:90%


01:03:31.630 --> 01:03:34.900 align:middle line:84%
basically, linear
transformations

01:03:34.900 --> 01:03:40.480 align:middle line:84%
don't change the skewness
of distributions.

01:03:40.480 --> 01:03:45.340 align:middle line:90%
And the kurtosis can--

01:03:45.340 --> 01:03:47.540 align:middle line:84%
well, it might
affect the kurtosis.

01:03:47.540 --> 01:03:48.100 align:middle line:90%
All right.

01:03:48.100 --> 01:03:51.700 align:middle line:84%
Well, let's move on
to considerations

01:03:51.700 --> 01:03:55.420 align:middle line:84%
of more than one random
variable and the concept of two

01:03:55.420 --> 01:03:59.370 align:middle line:84%
random variables
being independent.

01:03:59.370 --> 01:04:02.820 align:middle line:84%
In probability theory, we
say two random variables

01:04:02.820 --> 01:04:07.050 align:middle line:84%
are independent if
the joint probability

01:04:07.050 --> 01:04:16.150 align:middle line:84%
of the random variables,
X lying in a set A

01:04:16.150 --> 01:04:21.270 align:middle line:84%
and Y lying in a set B,
if that joint probability

01:04:21.270 --> 01:04:25.620 align:middle line:84%
of both occurring is equal
to just the product of each

01:04:25.620 --> 01:04:27.810 align:middle line:90%
occurring alone.

01:04:27.810 --> 01:04:36.490 align:middle line:84%
And so we have, for densities
or probability mass functions,

01:04:36.490 --> 01:04:41.160 align:middle line:84%
we have this property of the
joint density function factoring

01:04:41.160 --> 01:04:44.970 align:middle line:84%
into the product of the
marginal densities of each

01:04:44.970 --> 01:04:47.100 align:middle line:90%
of the random variables.

01:04:47.100 --> 01:04:52.890 align:middle line:84%
And it works for a continuous or
discrete distributions as well.

01:04:52.890 --> 01:04:59.600 align:middle line:84%
So independence means that,
basically, knowing one variable

01:04:59.600 --> 01:05:03.830 align:middle line:84%
doesn't give us any insight on
the value of the other variable.

01:05:03.830 --> 01:05:11.960 align:middle line:84%
Now, we can compute covariances
between two random variables

01:05:11.960 --> 01:05:14.670 align:middle line:90%
and correlations.

01:05:14.670 --> 01:05:27.710 align:middle line:84%
And so, with a covariance
between two random variables,

01:05:27.710 --> 01:05:32.910 align:middle line:84%
this is a generalization of
the variance for one variable.

01:05:32.910 --> 01:05:35.900 align:middle line:84%
But this is the
probability-weighted average

01:05:35.900 --> 01:05:38.030 align:middle line:90%
of x minus--

01:05:38.030 --> 01:05:43.720 align:middle line:84%
I'll write here mu x
times y minus mu y.

01:05:43.720 --> 01:05:46.400 align:middle line:90%


01:05:46.400 --> 01:05:53.770 align:middle line:84%
And the correlation
statistic corresponds

01:05:53.770 --> 01:06:05.230 align:middle line:84%
to dividing this
covariance by the product

01:06:05.230 --> 01:06:11.950 align:middle line:84%
of sigma x and sigma y, the
square root of the variances.

01:06:11.950 --> 01:06:16.780 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out
that this also can

01:06:16.780 --> 01:06:26.140 align:middle line:84%
be written as x minus mu x
over sigma x times y minus mu y

01:06:26.140 --> 01:06:29.110 align:middle line:90%
over sigma y.

01:06:29.110 --> 01:06:33.070 align:middle line:84%
And so the standardization
of the random variables

01:06:33.070 --> 01:06:40.670 align:middle line:84%
to have mean 0 and standard
deviation 1 can be made.

01:06:40.670 --> 01:06:44.710 align:middle line:84%
And then the expected product
of those standardized values

01:06:44.710 --> 01:06:48.936 align:middle line:84%
is the correlation statistic,
or the correlation parameter.

01:06:48.936 --> 01:06:51.510 align:middle line:90%


01:06:51.510 --> 01:06:58.440 align:middle line:84%
Importantly, zero correlation
doesn't imply independence.

01:06:58.440 --> 01:07:07.920 align:middle line:84%
But zero correlation does
mean that the dependence--

01:07:07.920 --> 01:07:11.010 align:middle line:84%
does suggest that there's
no linear dependence

01:07:11.010 --> 01:07:13.060 align:middle line:90%
between the two variables.

01:07:13.060 --> 01:07:15.990 align:middle line:84%
Now, when we
consider generalizing

01:07:15.990 --> 01:07:18.880 align:middle line:84%
from two random
variables to many,

01:07:18.880 --> 01:07:23.250 align:middle line:84%
then we can think of
working with random vectors.

01:07:23.250 --> 01:07:29.100 align:middle line:84%
And with, say, little
n being the count

01:07:29.100 --> 01:07:36.330 align:middle line:84%
of different random variables,
we can have notation of mu j

01:07:36.330 --> 01:07:39.330 align:middle line:84%
for the means or
expectations of each,

01:07:39.330 --> 01:07:46.020 align:middle line:84%
and sigma i, j corresponding to
the covariance between Xi and Xj

01:07:46.020 --> 01:07:48.330 align:middle line:90%
for sigma i, j.

01:07:48.330 --> 01:07:51.480 align:middle line:90%
And we can then represent--

01:07:51.480 --> 01:07:59.410 align:middle line:84%
the expected vector of x
values is equal to a mu vector.

01:07:59.410 --> 01:08:03.720 align:middle line:84%
And we can represent the
covariances between the elements

01:08:03.720 --> 01:08:09.000 align:middle line:84%
in terms of an
n-by-n matrix, sigma.

01:08:09.000 --> 01:08:13.860 align:middle line:84%
And so what's important
to realize here

01:08:13.860 --> 01:08:25.930 align:middle line:84%
is that if we have an x
vector and a mu vector,

01:08:25.930 --> 01:08:31.979 align:middle line:84%
so x is equal to x1
down to xn, and mu

01:08:31.979 --> 01:08:39.750 align:middle line:84%
is equal to mu 1
down to mu n, then

01:08:39.750 --> 01:08:44.670 align:middle line:84%
we define the
covariance matrix of x

01:08:44.670 --> 01:08:53.029 align:middle line:84%
to be the expected value of
x minus the mu vector times x

01:08:53.029 --> 01:08:56.760 align:middle line:90%
minus the mu vector transpose.

01:08:56.760 --> 01:08:58.950 align:middle line:90%
And so this is an n-by-1.

01:08:58.950 --> 01:09:02.640 align:middle line:90%
This is a 1-by-n transpose.

01:09:02.640 --> 01:09:07.250 align:middle line:84%
And so we get an
n-by-n matrix, where

01:09:07.250 --> 01:09:10.794 align:middle line:84%
we take expectations element
by element in that array.

01:09:10.794 --> 01:09:13.490 align:middle line:90%


01:09:13.490 --> 01:09:17.300 align:middle line:84%
So this is a nice
way of representing

01:09:17.300 --> 01:09:19.200 align:middle line:90%
the covariance matrix.

01:09:19.200 --> 01:09:24.620 align:middle line:84%
And what's useful in
particular is, well,

01:09:24.620 --> 01:09:28.490 align:middle line:84%
if we think of Xs maybe
representing returns

01:09:28.490 --> 01:09:32.899 align:middle line:84%
on different assets,
with means mu,

01:09:32.899 --> 01:09:38.000 align:middle line:84%
and the covariance matrix tells
us how much variability there is

01:09:38.000 --> 01:09:43.010 align:middle line:84%
in each of the Xi's, but also
the correlation structure across

01:09:43.010 --> 01:09:50.750 align:middle line:84%
the n Xs, then if
we consider, say,

01:09:50.750 --> 01:09:55.570 align:middle line:84%
a linear combination of
the Xs given by a vector

01:09:55.570 --> 01:10:02.260 align:middle line:84%
A of constants, then this
linear combination of the Xs can

01:10:02.260 --> 01:10:10.150 align:middle line:84%
actually represent maybe a
portfolio of holdings across

01:10:10.150 --> 01:10:12.740 align:middle line:84%
different assets
given by the Xi's.

01:10:12.740 --> 01:10:14.890 align:middle line:84%
And what we'd like
to be able to do

01:10:14.890 --> 01:10:21.140 align:middle line:84%
is model the mean value
of Y and its variance.

01:10:21.140 --> 01:10:26.530 align:middle line:84%
The mean value is very
simply the linear combination

01:10:26.530 --> 01:10:30.710 align:middle line:84%
of the component means
times the a coefficients.

01:10:30.710 --> 01:10:35.230 align:middle line:84%
And when we go to calculate
the variance of Y,

01:10:35.230 --> 01:10:37.480 align:middle line:84%
if you go through
each step here,

01:10:37.480 --> 01:10:43.060 align:middle line:84%
each equation here is doing
something slightly important.

01:10:43.060 --> 01:10:46.800 align:middle line:84%
We're basically substituting
in A transpose X for Y

01:10:46.800 --> 01:10:50.260 align:middle line:84%
at the beginning and a transpose
mu for the expected value.

01:10:50.260 --> 01:10:58.830 align:middle line:84%
Then we are able to pull out the
constant vector, a transpose,

01:10:58.830 --> 01:11:02.040 align:middle line:84%
in the front of the
expectation, and actually

01:11:02.040 --> 01:11:04.510 align:middle line:90%
in the back of the expectation.

01:11:04.510 --> 01:11:08.460 align:middle line:84%
So these coefficient
vectors are constants

01:11:08.460 --> 01:11:13.150 align:middle line:84%
and can be pulled out of the
expectation computations.

01:11:13.150 --> 01:11:16.770 align:middle line:84%
And then this middle term here
is simply the covariance matrix

01:11:16.770 --> 01:11:22.050 align:middle line:84%
of X. So we have that
the variance of Y

01:11:22.050 --> 01:11:27.960 align:middle line:84%
is simply the sum, double sum,
over all pairs of coefficients,

01:11:27.960 --> 01:11:33.480 align:middle line:84%
ai, aj, multiplied by the
covariance terms between i

01:11:33.480 --> 01:11:34.560 align:middle line:90%
and j.

01:11:34.560 --> 01:11:46.920 align:middle line:84%
So this is the formula
for the variance of Y.

01:11:46.920 --> 01:11:49.170 align:middle line:84%
I'm not sure if we go into
this in the next slide.

01:11:49.170 --> 01:11:57.510 align:middle line:84%
But if the covariance of
Xi and Xj is equal to 0--

01:11:57.510 --> 01:12:03.380 align:middle line:84%
so if this is equal to 0
for all i not equal to j,

01:12:03.380 --> 01:12:09.290 align:middle line:84%
then this variance
expression simplifies a lot.

01:12:09.290 --> 01:12:14.060 align:middle line:90%
And if, say, aij--

01:12:14.060 --> 01:12:24.890 align:middle line:84%
sorry, ai is equal to
aj is equal to 1 over n,

01:12:24.890 --> 01:12:35.150 align:middle line:84%
then the sum of the
ai's is equal to 1.

01:12:35.150 --> 01:12:45.240 align:middle line:84%
And the sum of the ai
squareds is equal to what?

01:12:45.240 --> 01:12:50.200 align:middle line:90%


01:12:50.200 --> 01:12:50.895 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: 1?

01:12:50.895 --> 01:12:51.770 align:middle line:90%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: No.

01:12:51.770 --> 01:12:54.702 align:middle line:90%
No, this is not equal to 1.

01:12:54.702 --> 01:12:55.910 align:middle line:90%
It's going to be less than 1.

01:12:55.910 --> 01:13:01.839 align:middle line:90%


01:13:01.839 --> 01:13:02.780 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: 1 over n.

01:13:02.780 --> 01:13:04.197 align:middle line:84%
PETER KEMPTHORNE:
1 over n, right.

01:13:04.197 --> 01:13:09.370 align:middle line:90%
It's n, n times 1 over n.

01:13:09.370 --> 01:13:12.820 align:middle line:84%
So it's-- sorry, n
times 1 over n squared.

01:13:12.820 --> 01:13:21.430 align:middle line:84%
So the variance of an equal
weighted average of the Xs

01:13:21.430 --> 01:13:25.900 align:middle line:84%
will have variance
that's 1 over n times

01:13:25.900 --> 01:13:27.740 align:middle line:90%
the variance of each of the Xs.

01:13:27.740 --> 01:13:31.300 align:middle line:84%
So this is where
diversification can

01:13:31.300 --> 01:13:36.310 align:middle line:84%
be useful in terms of
reducing variability.

01:13:36.310 --> 01:13:40.440 align:middle line:84%
And in the very
special case where

01:13:40.440 --> 01:13:49.050 align:middle line:84%
we have, say, zero correlated
assets and, I guess,

01:13:49.050 --> 01:13:54.330 align:middle line:84%
equal weighted
variances of the Xs,

01:13:54.330 --> 01:14:01.830 align:middle line:84%
then we can reduce the
variability by the factor n,

01:14:01.830 --> 01:14:04.440 align:middle line:84%
or multiplying by
the factor 1 over n.

01:14:04.440 --> 01:14:07.530 align:middle line:90%


01:14:07.530 --> 01:14:12.630 align:middle line:84%
Well, this leads us
to introducing you

01:14:12.630 --> 01:14:16.440 align:middle line:84%
to principal
components analysis.

01:14:16.440 --> 01:14:27.540 align:middle line:84%
And with principal
components analysis,

01:14:27.540 --> 01:14:34.013 align:middle line:84%
we consider an
m-variate vector x--

01:14:34.013 --> 01:14:40.820 align:middle line:90%


01:14:40.820 --> 01:14:43.220 align:middle line:84%
so we consider an
x vector that's

01:14:43.220 --> 01:14:56.000 align:middle line:84%
m by 1, which has an expected
value equal to an alpha vector,

01:14:56.000 --> 01:15:03.500 align:middle line:84%
and a covariance
matrix, which is m by m,

01:15:03.500 --> 01:15:08.120 align:middle line:90%
equal to sigma matrix.

01:15:08.120 --> 01:15:18.140 align:middle line:84%
And this covariance matrix is a
real matrix that is symmetric.

01:15:18.140 --> 01:15:23.300 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, it's going to
have real eigenvalues.

01:15:23.300 --> 01:15:32.780 align:middle line:84%
And it's also going to have
nonnegative eigenvalues

01:15:32.780 --> 01:15:41.080 align:middle line:84%
because the covariance matrix
is positive semidefinite.

01:15:41.080 --> 01:15:52.060 align:middle line:84%
And so sigma is
positive semidefinite.

01:15:52.060 --> 01:15:54.700 align:middle line:84%
The definition of
positive semidefinite

01:15:54.700 --> 01:16:02.920 align:middle line:84%
is that for all a
vectors, a transpose sigma

01:16:02.920 --> 01:16:05.963 align:middle line:90%
a is greater than or equal to 0.

01:16:05.963 --> 01:16:09.040 align:middle line:90%


01:16:09.040 --> 01:16:14.920 align:middle line:84%
And this is actually
the variance

01:16:14.920 --> 01:16:27.940 align:middle line:84%
of a transpose x, this product
of the covariance matrix

01:16:27.940 --> 01:16:30.290 align:middle line:84%
times the transpose of
an a vector and that.

01:16:30.290 --> 01:16:35.730 align:middle line:84%
So the fact that the variance
of the linear combination

01:16:35.730 --> 01:16:41.670 align:middle line:84%
of the x's is
strictly nonnegative

01:16:41.670 --> 01:16:47.430 align:middle line:84%
gives us this result here, which
means that the sigma matrix is

01:16:47.430 --> 01:16:49.860 align:middle line:90%
positive semidefinite.

01:16:49.860 --> 01:16:59.490 align:middle line:84%
And we can basically list the
eigenvalues to be lambda 1

01:16:59.490 --> 01:17:00.850 align:middle line:90%
through lambda m.

01:17:00.850 --> 01:17:04.860 align:middle line:90%
Those could be all different.

01:17:04.860 --> 01:17:10.410 align:middle line:84%
Some of them will be nonzero,
so long as there's no--

01:17:10.410 --> 01:17:19.060 align:middle line:84%
so long as the covariance
matrix has some positive rank.

01:17:19.060 --> 01:17:21.900 align:middle line:84%
And so, with this
framework, where

01:17:21.900 --> 01:17:29.580 align:middle line:84%
we have the covariance matrix,
sigma, having eigenvalues,

01:17:29.580 --> 01:17:39.390 align:middle line:84%
lambda i, and corresponding
eigenvectors, gamma sub i,

01:17:39.390 --> 01:17:42.780 align:middle line:84%
we can normalize the
eigenvectors, gamma i,

01:17:42.780 --> 01:17:47.850 align:middle line:90%
to be of unit length.

01:17:47.850 --> 01:17:51.790 align:middle line:84%
So gamma i transpose
gamma i is equal to 1.

01:17:51.790 --> 01:17:54.570 align:middle line:84%
And we can also
define the gammas

01:17:54.570 --> 01:17:58.440 align:middle line:90%
to be orthogonal to each other.

01:17:58.440 --> 01:18:08.580 align:middle line:84%
Then principal components
defines new variables by saying,

01:18:08.580 --> 01:18:11.790 align:middle line:90%
we take our vector x.

01:18:11.790 --> 01:18:17.010 align:middle line:84%
We transpose it
to x minus alpha.

01:18:17.010 --> 01:18:21.570 align:middle line:84%
So we're looking at deviations
from the mean values, component

01:18:21.570 --> 01:18:23.020 align:middle line:90%
by component.

01:18:23.020 --> 01:18:34.730 align:middle line:84%
And then we take each of
those, and we multiply them,

01:18:34.730 --> 01:18:39.923 align:middle line:84%
or look at the dot product
of gamma i dot x minus alpha.

01:18:39.923 --> 01:18:42.920 align:middle line:90%


01:18:42.920 --> 01:18:51.470 align:middle line:84%
And we call this the coefficient
of the i-th principal component

01:18:51.470 --> 01:18:53.720 align:middle line:90%
variables.

01:18:53.720 --> 01:18:57.320 align:middle line:84%
And these principal
component variables

01:18:57.320 --> 01:19:06.050 align:middle line:84%
are very neat because
their average or mean

01:19:06.050 --> 01:19:09.053 align:middle line:90%
value is equal to 0.

01:19:09.053 --> 01:19:12.140 align:middle line:90%


01:19:12.140 --> 01:19:17.826 align:middle line:84%
But the covariance matrix
between the p's is--

01:19:17.826 --> 01:19:20.030 align:middle line:90%
[ALARM RINGING]

01:19:20.030 --> 01:19:22.165 align:middle line:84%
It's time to finish
the lecture here.

01:19:22.165 --> 01:19:25.130 align:middle line:90%


01:19:25.130 --> 01:19:31.370 align:middle line:84%
The covariance matrix is
the diagonal matrix lambda.

01:19:31.370 --> 01:19:38.860 align:middle line:84%
So we basically have transformed
our original x vector

01:19:38.860 --> 01:19:46.600 align:middle line:84%
into new coordinates,
which have mean 0,

01:19:46.600 --> 01:19:49.430 align:middle line:84%
are uncorrelated
with each other,

01:19:49.430 --> 01:19:53.680 align:middle line:84%
and have variability that
decreases, basically,

01:19:53.680 --> 01:20:00.760 align:middle line:84%
with i, if we rank the principal
components, eigenvectors, lambda

01:20:00.760 --> 01:20:02.770 align:middle line:90%
1, to be bigger than--

01:20:02.770 --> 01:20:07.160 align:middle line:84%
or basically, to be ordered
from lambda 1 to lambda m.

01:20:07.160 --> 01:20:11.890 align:middle line:84%
So we have an orthogonal basis
for our data in this case.

01:20:11.890 --> 01:20:17.930 align:middle line:84%
And I guess we'll
finish there for today.

01:20:17.930 --> 01:20:21.050 align:middle line:84%
But on Thursday, we'll
finish these notes up.

01:20:21.050 --> 01:20:25.470 align:middle line:84%
And what you'll
be able to see is

01:20:25.470 --> 01:20:34.870 align:middle line:84%
how we can try to represent
an x vector as a sum of terms.

01:20:34.870 --> 01:20:37.860 align:middle line:84%
And maybe the sum
of terms corresponds

01:20:37.860 --> 01:20:42.060 align:middle line:84%
to using just a small number
of the principal component

01:20:42.060 --> 01:20:43.000 align:middle line:90%
variables.

01:20:43.000 --> 01:20:47.100 align:middle line:84%
So when we have
multidimensional vectors that

01:20:47.100 --> 01:20:52.320 align:middle line:84%
have variability that
is limited to a smaller

01:20:52.320 --> 01:20:56.400 align:middle line:84%
number of dimensions, we can
characterize those smaller

01:20:56.400 --> 01:21:00.060 align:middle line:84%
number of dimensions nicely
with principal components

01:21:00.060 --> 01:21:03.760 align:middle line:90%
and obtain useful models.

01:21:03.760 --> 01:21:08.490 align:middle line:84%
And these kinds of models
underlie multifactor models

01:21:08.490 --> 01:21:14.020 align:middle line:84%
often in modeling asset
returns and financial markets.

01:21:14.020 --> 01:21:19.610 align:middle line:84%
So we'll finish there
for today, and thank you.

01:21:19.610 --> 01:21:25.000 align:middle line:90%